    "narrative": "Our primary objective today is to exploit soft matchups against middle-of-the-road starters, with **Tyler Freeman** leading the charge at a 69.79 score. Facing Leiter, **Tyler Freeman** benefits from a massive 14% park boost, elevating his utility in the three-hole to our top projected play. **Luis Arraez** (67.62) and **Matt Chapman** (64.54) follow closely, both capitalizing on a prime matchup against Me. Kelly. While **Matt Chapman** usually relies on power, the model identifies a specific edge here: the slow delivery of the opposition provides a rare 2% boost to his stolen base expectancy\u2014a critical nuance in our 5x5 Roto context where every stolen base is prioritized. Similarly, **Will Smith** (64.07) serves as a strong anchor in the catcher spot, aided significantly by facing the vulnerable 4.94 xERA of V\u00e1squez.\n\nThe most difficult decision on today's card was limiting the exposure of **Masyn Winn**. Despite a respectable 46.27 score, **Masyn Winn** was squeezed out of the starting nine by the superior situational efficiency of **Daulton Varsho** (55.17) and **Bryson Stott** (52.97). The primary limiting factor for **Masyn Winn** was a defensive efficiency penalty tied to an opposing catcher's elite 1.86s pop time, which suppresses his aggressive base-running strategy. Elsewhere, we are exercising caution with **Gavin Sheets** (42.96); while he secures a spot, his matchup against Ohtani is a genuine concern, as the 2.75 xERA represents a \"Below floor\" ceiling for any hitter, making him the most likely candidate for a mid-game hook if the score slips further.\n\nBeyond these selections, we continue to monitor the status of **Cal Raleigh** and **Yainer Diaz**, who remain on the shelf, leaving our catching depth thin. While we are maximizing our output today with **Taylor Ward** and **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**, their scores are currently dampened by facing tough left-handed starters; should weather patterns shift or bullpens get involved early, we are prepared to pivot to the bench if the efficiency gap narrows against our projected starters. We remain committed to the speed-first philosophy, keeping **Drew Gilbert** in the lineup primarily for his latent stolen base potential despite the lineup-order penalty at #9.",
